# What this machine is called, declared — because the name is what `binding`
# matches on, and matching is how a checkout learns which profile it wears.
#
# THIS IS INTENT, NOT A MIRROR. The machine is asked at run time; this file is
# what the answer is supposed to be, and `../binding` reports when they differ.
#
# THREE COLUMNS, AND THE FIRST IS THE PLATFORM. macOS has a `ComputerName` and
# Windows has a `COMPUTERNAME` and they are not the same field — one is a
# display string with spaces and an apostrophe in it, the other is a
# NetBIOS-flavoured label. Sharing a key would let one host match the other's
# name. This is the file that made that a real risk rather than a theoretical
# one, because it is the first non-macOS profile here.
#
# UNFILLED. Nobody has read this off the box yet. Until somebody does,
# `machines/binding` matches nothing here and reports the checkout as
# unclaimed — which is correct, and is the clean state rather than a fault.
#
# To fill it, on the machine:
#
#     echo %COMPUTERNAME%          :: cmd
#     $env:COMPUTERNAME            # PowerShell
#
# and put the answer below. Case does not matter — `binding` compares
# case-insensitively, because hostnames are case-insensitive and the
# alternative is a file that has to be typed in the same shift state as the
# dialog box it came from.

# platform   key             value
# windows    ComputerName
